{"text":"Focus component: model_design. Use Tier 2 architectural deepening by adding content-dependent gates to the existing causal dilated-convolution blocks, allowing each block to control which sequence features enter its residual update. The positional-removal experiment reduced validation HR@10 from 0.650 to 0.645, so retain positional embeddings and use the 0.650 parent as the starting point. A plausible remaining limitation is that its fixed GELU transformation cannot explicitly suppress irrelevant historical features according to the current causal context; this is a hypothesis for the next controlled experiment.

Make one architectural change inside NextItNetBlock: expand conv1 to produce twice hidden_units channels, split its output into feature and gate tensors, and replace the existing activation output with GELU(features) multiplied by 2 * sigmoid(gates). Initialize the gate half of conv1 weights and biases to zero so the gate initially multiplies features by one, then learns context-dependent modulation through the existing BPR objective. Apply the same left-only causal padding to both halves, and preserve conv2, normalization, dropout, learned residual scales, positional embeddings, masking, dilation schedule, tied item scoring, and all public interfaces. Keep all eight numeric constants unchanged and retain exactly the permitted imports and two classes, with no model-side training, evaluation, logging, or file operations.

Allocate one of the 44 candidate slots to this gated NextItNet and let the unchanged frozen host train and evaluate it against the 0.650 parent using validation HR@10. Retain the candidate only if that metric improves; this directly tests adaptive filtering within the required convolutional paradigm, without combining the failed positional ablation, changing host behavior, or consulting a test metric."}
